    "clinical": "<p><strong>Clinical Case Study: Extrahepatic Biliary Obstruction (Choledocholithiasis) in a Cocker Spaniel</strong><br><strong>Species & Signalment:</strong> Canine — 9-year-old female spayed Cocker Spaniel, 12 kg<br><strong>History & Signs:</strong> Presented with acute onset of severe depression, vomiting, anorexia, and progressive yellow discoloration of the sclera and oral mucous membranes for 4 days. Stool passed was grayish-white and greasy.<br><strong>Biochemical Findings:</strong> Total Bilirubin: 9.4 mg/dL (Ref: 0.1-0.5); Conjugated Bilirubin: 7.6 mg/dL (Ref: 0.0-0.2); ALP: 2450 U/L (Ref: 20-150); GGT: 180 U/L (Ref: 0-10); ALT: 310 U/L (Ref: 15-100); AST: 140 U/L (Ref: 15-50); Cholesterol: 480 mg/dL (Ref: 130-300). Urinalysis: Bilirubin 3+; Urobilinogen: Negative. Abdominal ultrasound revealed marked dilation of the common bile duct with an acoustic shadowing cholelith at the duodenal papilla.<br><strong>Diagnostic Interpretation:</strong> Severe Post-Hepatic (Obstructive) Cholestasis. The direct (conjugated) bilirubin fraction accounts for > 80% of total bilirubin, supported by massive elevations in inducible canalicular enzymes (ALP, GGT) and negative urine urobilinogen with acholic feces.<br><strong>Therapeutic Management:</strong> Immediate fluid stabilization with IV balanced crystalloids, parenteral Vitamin K1 administration (to correct coagulopathy from malabsorbed fat-soluble vitamins), and emergency surgical exploratory laparotomy with choledochotomy and biliary decompression.</p><p><strong>Clinical Case Study: Subacute Aflatoxicosis and Toxic Hepatopathy in a Jersey Heifer</strong><br><strong>Species & Signalment:</strong> Bovine — 2-year-old pregnant Jersey cross heifer, 340 kg<br><strong>History & Signs:</strong> Herd outbreak following consumption of moldy groundnut cake feed. Presented with dullness, dropped milk yield, submandibular edema, rectal tenesmus, and mild icteric sclera.<br><strong>Biochemical Findings:</strong> Total Bilirubin: 3.8 mg/dL (Ref: 0.1-0.5); Conjugated: 1.9 mg/dL; Unconjugated: 1.9 mg/dL (Biphasic); SDH: 98 U/L (Ref: 5-25); GLDH: 145 U/L (Ref: 2-15); GGT: 110 U/L (Ref: 10-35); Serum Albumin: 2.1 g/dL (Ref: 3.0-4.0); Prothrombin Time: 28 sec (Ref: 12-16 sec). Feed analysis confirmed aflatoxin B1 level of 1.2 ppm.<br><strong>Diagnostic Interpretation:</strong> Acute-on-chronic Hepatic (Hepatocellular) Toxicosis due to Aflatoxin B1 epoxidation. Biphasic hyperbilirubinemia, dramatic release of centrilobular GLDH/SDH, hypoalbuminemia, and coagulopathy confirm severe loss of hepatocellular synthetic and functional mass.<br><strong>Therapeutic Management:</strong> Immediately remove contaminated feed; administer activated charcoal and bentonite clay (mycotoxin binders) orally; provide IV dextrose and choline/methionine lipotropic liver support; administer parenteral Vitamin K1 and broad-spectrum antibiotics to curb secondary bacteremia.</p>"
