    "clinical": "<p><strong>Clinical Case Study: Immune-Complex Glomerulonephritis and Nephrotic Syndrome in a Golden Retriever</strong><br><strong>Species & Signalment:</strong> Canine — 6-year-old intact male Golden Retriever, 32 kg<br><strong>History & Signs:</strong> Presented with progressive bilateral pitting edema of both hindlimbs, ascites, lethargy, and mild weight loss over 3 weeks. Appetite was mildly reduced.<br><strong>Biochemical Findings:</strong> Serum Albumin: 1.4 g/dL (Ref: 2.6-3.8); Total Serum Protein: 3.8 g/dL (Ref: 5.5-7.5); Total Serum Cholesterol: 540 mg/dL (Ref: 130-300); BUN: 28 mg/dL (Ref: 7-27); Serum Creatinine: 1.2 mg/dL (Ref: 0.5-1.4). Urinalysis (Cystocentesis): USG: 1.026; Protein: 4+ (> 500 mg/dL); Blood: Neg; Sediment: 2-3 hyaline casts/LPF, no cells. UPC Ratio: 8.6 (Ref: < 0.2).<br><strong>Diagnostic Interpretation:</strong> Nephrotic Syndrome secondary to severe Glomerulonephritis. The diagnostic tetrad of severe selective hypoalbuminemia, massive proteinuria (UPC 8.6), hypercholesterolemia, and cavitary edema/ascites without azotemia establishes primary glomerular capillary wall breakdown.<br><strong>Therapeutic Management:</strong> Initiate ACE inhibitor (Enalapril or Benazepril) to reduce intraglomerular capillary pressure; initiate low-dose aspirin or Clopidogrel to prevent pulmonary thromboembolism (secondary to urinary loss of Antithrombin III); provide renal therapeutic diet and spironolactone for ascites control.</p><p><strong>Clinical Case Study: Ethylene Glycol (Antifreeze) Toxicosis and Acute Renal Failure in a Cat</strong><br><strong>Species & Signalment:</strong> Feline — 3-year-old male neutered Domestic Shorthair, 4.2 kg<br><strong>History & Signs:</strong> Outdoor cat found severely depressed, hypothermic ($96.5^\\circ\\text{F}$), vomiting, and anuric. Owner noted garage access where vehicle radiator coolant was stored.<br><strong>Biochemical Findings:</strong> BUN: 142 mg/dL (Ref: 15-34); Creatinine: 11.8 mg/dL (Ref: 0.8-2.1); Phosphorus: 14.5 mg/dL (Ref: 3.0-6.0); Potassium: 7.2 mEq/L (Ref: 3.5-5.5); Serum Bicarbonate: 8 mEq/L (Severe metabolic acidosis; Anion Gap: 34 mEq/L). Urinalysis (Cystocentesis): USG: 1.010 (Isosthenuria); pH: 5.0; Glucose: 2+; Protein: 2+; Sediment: Innumerable 'picket-fence' and dumbbell-shaped Calcium Oxalate Monohydrate crystals and abundant coarse granular casts.<br><strong>Diagnostic Interpretation:</strong> Acute Intrinsic Renal Failure (Acute Tubular Necrosis) secondary to Ethylene Glycol Toxicosis. Hepatic alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes ethylene glycol to glycolic and oxalic acids, which form insoluble calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals that physically obstruct and mechanically shred renal tubules.<br><strong>Therapeutic Management:</strong> Immediate emergency intensive care: IV balanced isotonic fluid therapy; IV 20% ethanol infusion or 4-methylpyrazole (Fomepizole, though far less effective in cats once azotemia is established); IV sodium bicarbonate for profound acidemia; peritoneal dialysis or hemodialysis for anuric renal arrest.</p>"
