    "clinical": "<p><strong>Clinical Case Study: Exocrine Pancreatic Insufficiency (EPI) with SIBO in a German Shepherd Dog</strong><br><strong>Species & Signalment:</strong> Canine — 2-year-old intact male German Shepherd Dog, 21 kg (body condition score 2/9)<br><strong>History & Signs:</strong> Presented with progressive, severe weight loss over 4 months despite an insatiable, ravenous appetite (coprophagic). The dog passes 5–7 voluminous, semi-formed, putty-colored, extremely rancid-smelling stools daily.<br><strong>Biochemical Findings:</strong> Canine cTLI: 0.8 $\\mu$g/L (Ref: 5.7-45.2; Diagnostic for EPI); Serum Cobalamin (Vitamin B12): 110 ng/L (Severe deficiency; Ref: 250-900); Serum Folate: 22.4 $\\mu$g/L (Elevated; Ref: 5.0-13.0); Serum Albumin: 2.8 g/dL; Total Protein: 5.6 g/dL.<br><strong>Diagnostic Interpretation:</strong> Severe Exocrine Pancreatic Insufficiency (EPI) secondary to Pancreatic Acinar Atrophy (PAA), accompanied by secondary Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth (SIBO). Deficient pancreatic intrinsic factor caused profound hypocobalaminemia, while bacterial overgrowth synthesized excessive folate.<br><strong>Therapeutic Management:</strong> Lifelong dietary supplementation of powdered pancreatic enzyme replacement (Pancreatin / Viokase) mixed thoroughly with meals; weekly subcutaneous cyanocobalamin injections (500 $\\mu$g SC once weekly for 6 weeks); temporary 4-week course of oral tylosin to resolve SIBO.</p><p><strong>Clinical Case Study: Strangulating Small Intestinal Volvulus in a Thoroughbred Broodmare</strong><br><strong>Species & Signalment:</strong> Equine — 8-year-old Thoroughbred broodmare, 520 kg<br><strong>History & Signs:</strong> Presented with violent, refractory colic of 5 hours duration: pawing, rolling, sweating, heart rate 84 bpm, toxic congested mucous membranes with a distinct capillary toxic line. Unresponsive to flunixin meglumine and xylazine analgesia.<br><strong>Biochemical Findings:</strong> Venous Blood: Lactate: 4.8 mmol/L (Ref: 0.5-1.5); Hematocrit: 52%; Total Protein: 7.8 g/dL. Abdominocentesis: Peritoneal Fluid: Color: Serosanguinous (turbid dark red); Total Protein: 5.2 g/dL (Ref: < 2.5); Total Nucleated Cell Count: 38,000 /$\\mu$L; Peritoneal Lactate: 11.6 mmol/L (Ref: < 1.5; Peritoneal-to-blood lactate ratio = 2.4).<br><strong>Diagnostic Interpretation:</strong> Strangulating Intestinal Obstruction (Small Intestinal Volvulus). Markedly elevated peritoneal lactate (11.6 mmol/L) and a ratio > 2.0 relative to blood lactate establish transmural ischemic intestinal necrosis.<br><strong>Therapeutic Management:</strong> Immediate emergency exploratory celiotomy; extensive small intestinal resection and jejunojejunostomy anastomosis; aggressive perioperative IV balanced crystalloids and flunixin meglumine for endotoxemic support.</p>"
