        "title": "Severe Hypovolemic Shock and Dehydration from Canine Parvovirus",
        "species": "Canine",
        "signalment": "4-month-old unvaccinated male Rottweiler puppy, 10 kg",
        "history": "Presented with a 3-day history of profuse, hemorrhagic foul-smelling diarrhea, intractable vomiting, and anorexia. The puppy was recumbent, unresponsive, hypothermic ($97.0^\\circ\\text{F}$), with dry cyanotic mucous membranes, CRT 4 seconds, and a persistent skin tent.",
        "biochemFindings": "Blood Lactate: 6.2 mmol/L; $Na^+$: 131 mEq/L; $K^+$: 2.8 mEq/L; $Cl^-$: 95 mEq/L; $HCO_3^-$: 12 mEq/L; PCV: 58%; Total Protein: 4.2 g/dL. Clinical dehydration assessed at 10%.",
        "interpretation": "Severe Hypovolemic Shock and 10% Dehydration with Hypokalemia and Metabolic Acidosis secondary to Parvoviral Enteritis. Intestinal fluid and protein loss produced severe hypoperfusion and low oncotic pressure.",
        "action": "Immediate emergency IV resuscitation: administered isotonic crystalloid (Normosol-R) shock bolus of $20\\text{ mL/kg}$ ($200\\text{ mL}$) over 15 minutes; followed by calculation of 24-hr fluid needs: Deficit ($10\\text{ kg} \\times 10\\% \\times 10 = 1000\\text{ mL}$) + Maintenance ($60\\text{ mL/kg/day} = 600\\text{ mL}$) + Ongoing losses ($400\\text{ mL}$) = $2000\\text{ mL/day}$ ($83\\text{ mL/hr}$). Supplemented with $30\\text{ mEq/L } KCl$ (ensuring infusion rate $< 0.5\\text{ mEq/kg/hr}$) and broad-spectrum IV antibiotics."
