        "history": "Presented with severe tachypnea, open-mouth breathing, hypothermia ($96.8^\\circ\\text{F}$), severe facial and paw edema, and marked cyanosis. The owner had administered half of a human 500 mg paracetamol tablet (~250 mg, or ~65 mg/kg) 6 hours earlier for mild limping.",
        "biochemFindings": "Venous Blood: Characteristic dark 'chocolate-brown' color that failed to turn bright red upon oxygen bubbling. Methemoglobin concentration: 42% (Ref: < 2%). Blood smear showed 35% Heinz body formation on new methylene blue stain. Serum ALT: 480 U/L (Centrilobular hepatic injury; Ref: 15-100); Total Bilirubin: 2.1 mg/dL.",
        "interpretation": "Acute Acetaminophen Toxicosis with Severe Methemoglobinemia and Heinz Body Hemolytic Anemia. Feline deficiency of UGT1A6 shunted paracetamol to CYP2E1, generating toxic NAPQI, which depleted erythrocyte and hepatic glutathione, oxidizing hemoglobin iron to ferric $Fe^{3+}$.",
        "action": "Immediate emergency administration of N-Acetylcysteine (NAC): loading dose of $140\\text{ mg/kg}$ IV, followed by $70\\text{ mg/kg}$ IV every 4 hours for 7 doses; concurrent Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C, $30\\text{ mg/kg}$) to chemically reduce methemoglobin; oxygen cage therapy; S-Adenosylmethionine (SAMe) for ongoing hepatoprotection."
