    "clinical": "<p><strong>Clinical Case Study: Delayed Anesthetic Recovery from Propofol in a Racing Greyhound (CYP2B11)</strong><br><strong>Species & Signalment:</strong> Canine — 3-year-old female intact Racing Greyhound, 29 kg<br><strong>History & Signs:</strong> Underwent minor surgical repair of a fractured right accessory carpal bone. Anesthesia was induced with IV Propofol ($5\\text{ mg/kg}$) and maintained with isoflurane for 30 minutes. Four hours post-operatively, the dog remained recumbent, severely ataxic, hypothermic ($97.4^\\circ\\text{F}$), and unable to lift its head.<br><strong>Biochemical Findings:</strong> Routine pre-anesthetic CBC and serum chemistry panels were completely normal (ALT: 28 U/L, BUN: 14 mg/dL, Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL, Albumin: 3.2 g/dL). Blood glucose: 95 mg/dL. No focal neurological deficits detected.<br><strong>Diagnostic Interpretation:</strong> Prolonged Post-Anesthetic Recovery secondary to Deficient Hepatic CYP2B11 Clearance of Propofol. Greyhounds exhibit uniquely slow oxidative hydroxylation of propofol by CYP2B11, coupled with their low percentage of body fat (which reduces tissue redistribution capacity).<br><strong>Therapeutic Management:</strong> Provide active external warming (forced-air warmers); supportive IV balanced crystalloids; maintain sternal recumbency and patient turning every 2 hours until spontaneous hepatic drug clearance occurs (complete recovery achieved at 8 hours); record permanent anesthetic warning against thiobarbiturates and prolonged propofol infusions.</p><p><strong>Clinical Case Study: Phenobarbital Auto-Induction and Breakthrough Seizures in an Epileptic Beagle</strong><br><strong>Species & Signalment:</strong> Canine — 4-year-old male neutered Beagle, 14 kg<br><strong>History & Signs:</strong> Diagnosed with idiopathic epilepsy 6 weeks ago and initiated on oral Phenobarbital at $3\\text{ mg/kg}$ twice daily. Initial seizure control was excellent, but over the past week the dog experienced three breakthrough generalized tonic-clonic seizures.<br><strong>Biochemical Findings:</strong> Serum Phenobarbital Concentration: 14 $\\mu$g/mL (Subtherapeutic; Therapeutic Target: 20-35 $\\mu$g/mL). At 2 weeks post-initiation, the level was 24 $\\mu$g/mL. Serum ALP: 340 U/L (Mild induction; Ref: 20-150); ALT: 45 U/L (Normal).<br><strong>Diagnostic Interpretation:</strong> Therapeutic Failure secondary to Phenobarbital Hepatic Enzyme Auto-Induction. Chronic phenobarbital activates the nuclear CAR and PXR receptors, stimulating de novo transcription of CYP2B, CYP3A, and POR enzymes. This accelerated the drug's own clearance, causing circulating blood levels to drop below the therapeutic threshold.<br><strong>Therapeutic Management:</strong> Increase oral Phenobarbital dosage by 25–30% (to $4\\text{ mg/kg}$ twice daily); recheck serum phenobarbital trough concentration in 3 weeks; counsel owner that periodic monitoring is mandatory to compensate for ongoing microsomal enzyme induction.</p>"
